;; Opening a POP3 maildrop and listing it, as a Kotoba application
;; (ADR-2608261100). This is the vertical slice, not another judgement table:
;; the contract is the one `examples/todo-app.kotoba` and
;; `org-ietf-smtp/kotoba/smtp/session.kotoba` use —
;;
;;   init     (config)       -> state
;;   step     (state, line)  -> state    ; one reply line in
;;   closed   (state)        -> state    ; the host saw EOF
;;   outgoing (state)        -> string   ; the inert effect out
;;
;; State is a `:document`, and so is the `config` handed to `init`: `:user`,
;; `:has-password?`, `:has-access-token?`, `:uidl?`, and `:want`
;; (`:list`, the default, or `:retrieve` with `:number`).
;;
;; **No credential reaches the guest.** `init` is told *whether* a password
;; and a token exist, never what they are. The effect channel is a pair:
;; `outgoing-kind` says what the host must write and `outgoing` carries the
;; line when the guest can write it in full. Four kinds name a credential the
;; host holds and the guest does not:
;;
;;   :line                     write `outgoing` verbatim
;;   :none                     write nothing
;;   :credential/pass          write `PASS <password>` CRLF
;;   :credential/apop          write `APOP <user> <md5(stamp,secret)>` CRLF
;;   :credential/sasl-plain    write base64(NUL user NUL password) CRLF
;;   :credential/sasl-xoauth2  write base64(the XOAUTH2 blob) CRLF
;;
;; This is not a workaround, and it is not only about secrets being secret.
;; The guest's product decision is *which mechanism a server's advertisement
;; makes preferable, and what each reply means at each step*. Assembling
;; `NUL user NUL password` is credential handling, which is exactly the
;; ambient authority the `:authority` shielding axis names
;; (adr-2608301500). The socket, TLS, STLS upgrade, base64 and MD5 are
;; mechanism for the same reason.
;;
;; It also removes a byte this file cannot honestly contain. Measured
;; 2026-08-30 on amu 88ae83e: the Kotoba reader has no `\uXXXX` escape —
;; `"\u0000"` is rejected `:kotoba/source-read-failed`, and a **raw** NUL
;; byte is accepted. So SASL PLAIN's separator could only have been written
;; as a raw control byte, which is what the superproject's
;; `verify-source-nul-bytes` detector reports and which makes `grep` answer
;; about this file as though a word were absent. A guest that does not build
;; credentials never needs the byte.
;;
;; What `+OK` and `-ERR` mean, when a multi-line response has ended, that a
;; body line beginning `..` was stuffed, and that LIST and UIDL must be
;; joined on the message number are product semantics and live here.
;;
;; The oracle is `pop3.client/connect!` + `authenticate!` + `list-messages!`,
;; which carry four RFC rules this module reproduces:
;;
;;   * CAPA answering `-ERR` is not a failure (RFC 2449) — it means a plain
;;     RFC 1939 server, so capabilities are empty and the session proceeds;
;;   * UIDL is in RFC 1939's optional-commands section, so a server without
;;     it yields listings with an absent `:uid`, not a failed listing;
;;   * the message *number* does not survive the session and the UID does
;;     (§7), so a listing that reports only numbers is not the same answer;
;;   * a body line sent as `..` was byte-stuffed (§3) and must lose one dot.
;;
;; `throw` is not in the language (ADR-2608650000: permanent, and refined
;; 2026-08-30 by adr-2608301500 to "no UNTRACKED control effects" — the
;; ambient form stays rejected on every backend). The oracle's five
;; `ex-info` sites become the `:failed` phase plus `error-text`. The messages
;; are byte-identical to the oracle's so the parity test compares them
;; directly.
;;
;; ## Two deliberate design decisions, both measured
;;
;; **One module, not four.** `org-ietf-smtp` split its slice across four
;; namespaces and recorded the cost in its own header: the CLI's
;; multi-module route reaches only `js-browser` (measured 2026-08-28 on amu
;; 82c7e064 — `compile --module-lock … --target wasm32-browser` answers
;; `:usage`). POP3's surface fits one file, so this compiles through the
;; single-file route, which is JVM-free (`bin/amu`'s `nbbNativeTargets`
;; covers `wasm32-browser`; project mode does not and fails closed under
;; `--jvm-free`). Q9 forbids a JVM build dependency, so the single-module
;; form is not a stylistic preference here. Removal: nothing to remove —
;; when `compile --module-lock` accepts a non-JS target this file may split
;; if it ever outgrows one module.
;;
;; **RETR passes bytes through; it does not hold them.** A retrieved message
;; is bytes, and a `:document` cannot hold them. The bound that binds first
;; is `:container-items 32`, so a guest collecting body lines traps
;; `document-vector-too-large` on the 33rd line of an ordinary mail --
;; measured, by making it accumulate and watching the control below fail
;; with exactly that trap. (`:nodes 256` and `:aggregate-utf8-bytes 65536`
;; are the other ceilings; 32 is the one a message meets first.) The principle
;; ADR-2608039970 states for the datom plane applies here: bytes do not go
;; through the value plane. So the guest owns *when the body starts, when
;; the terminator ends it, and whether a line was stuffed* — and hands the
;; host one unstuffed line at a time, keeping none of them:
;;
;;   body-state  :none -> :open -> :closed   ; the host opens and seals a buffer
;;   body-line?                              ; did THIS step produce a line
;;   body-line                               ; the line to append
;;
;; `retrieved-bytes` counts what went past, which is semantics (the caller
;; asked for a message and is owed an answer about it) and costs one i64.
;; The message itself is never a guest value. `session-carries-no-message-
;; bytes` is the negative control: it drives a 2000-line body past every one
;; of those bounds, which only completes because nothing here accumulates
;; it.
;;
;; CAPA / LIST / UIDL are accumulated because they are bounded and
;; structured — they are semantics, not payload.
;;
;; No regular expressions: the oracle's four `re-find` sites became
;; positional scans (ADR-2608650000 decision 6 — redesign the scan, do not
;; wait for the operation). `org-ietf-smtp/kotoba/smtp/protocol_response`
;; is the precedent.
;;
;; `.cljc` remains the oracle and is not required from here (require-graph).
;;
;; ## Type annotations: 25 of the 217 this file used to carry
;;
;; They were written when an absent annotation meant `:i64`, so anything
;; touching a string or a document had to say so. The frontend now infers a
;; parameter's type from what its body requires and a result's from what its
;; body returns, and 192 of the 217 said nothing it did not already know.
;;
;; Established by measurement, not by reading: each annotation was removed
;; one at a time and the removal kept only when the whole module's signature
;; map -- 83 functions, every parameter type and every result -- came back
;; identical. The emitted wasm32 is byte-identical either way.
;;
;; The 25 that stayed were measured too: removing any one of them alone is
;; refused. Three reasons, and only the first is about this file.
;;
;;   * A function whose result feeds its own recursive call has no result to
;;     infer from until it has one. Eleven of these -- `trim-right`,
;;     `i64->string`, `ci-eq-from?`, `ci-has-token?`, `stamp-from`,
;;     `capa-sasl`, `capa-has?`, `uid-for`, `scan-listing`, `uidl-listing`,
;;     `join-uids`. Without the annotation the checker answers `if branches
;;     must have the same value type`.
;;
;;   * `dg`'s key. `document-get` takes a keyword OR a document, so nothing
;;     in the body pins `k` and the callers' keywords meet a provisional
;;     `:i64`.
;;
;;   * The rest are the frontend's shape, not this file's. Parameters are
;;     inferred before results (`frontend.cljc`: "Parameters before
;;     results"), a body's inference stops at its first unresolved form, and
;;     only a complaint naming a bare parameter drives a refinement -- so a
;;     parameter whose requirement lies past a call to a still-unannotated
;;     helper is never reached. Result inference is two passes, which is one
;;     short for `on-status` and `step`: a six-pass copy of the same frontend
;;     infers both. Re-measure these when the frontend moves; they are the
;;     annotations most likely to stop being necessary.
;;
;; Compile (the CLI needs -M, an ABSOLUTE path, `--output`, and the
;; `wasm32-browser` / `js-browser` target names — ADR-2608262000):
;;   kotoba -M compile /ABS/kotoba/pop3/session.kotoba \
;;     --target wasm32-browser --output session.wasm
;;
;; Measured 2026-08-30 on amu 88ae83e, both without a JDK on PATH:
;;   amu check   … --jvm-free                     -> :ok true, :effects #{}
;;   amu compile … --jvm-free --target wasm32-browser
;;                                                -> 12554-byte .wasm
;; Those are observations of one build, not a contract; re-measure rather
;; than quoting them.

(ns pop3.session
  (:export [init step closed
            phase outgoing outgoing-kind error-text
            body-state body-line? body-line retrieved-bytes
            apop-timestamp
            message-count message-number-at message-size-at message-uid-at
            drop-count drop-size
            supports? sasl-plain? sasl-xoauth2?]))

;; --- document accessors ---------------------------------------------------

(defn- dg [d k :keyword]
  (option-value-of [:option :document] (document-get d k) (document-null)))

(defn- ds [d k]
  (option-value-of [:option :string] (document-string-value (dg d k)) ""))

(defn- di [d k]
  (option-value-of [:option :i64] (document-i64-value (dg d k)) -1))

(defn- dk [d k]
  (option-value-of [:option :keyword] (document-keyword-value (dg d k)) :none))

(defn- db [d k]
  (option-value-of [:option :bool] (document-bool-value (dg d k)) false))

(defn- at [v i]
  (option-value-of [:option :document] (document-vector-at v i) (document-null)))

(defn- at-str [v i]
  (option-value-of [:option :string] (document-string-value (at v i)) ""))

;; --- byte scanning --------------------------------------------------------
;; `string-substring` indexes are checked UTF-8 byte boundaries, and POP3's
;; wire grammar (RFC 1939 §3) is US-ASCII, so byte offsets are the right
;; unit for the scans below. Message *text* is not scanned here at all.

(defn- space? [c] (or (= c 32) (= c 9)))

(defn- digit? [c] (and (>= c 48) (<= c 57)))

(defn- at-byte [s i]
  (if (< i (string-byte-length s)) (string-code-point-at s i) -1))

(defn- starts-with? [s prefix]
  (let [n (string-byte-length prefix)]
    (if (> n (string-byte-length s))
      false
      (string=? (string-substring s 0 n) prefix))))

(defn- skip-spaces [s i]
  (if (space? (at-byte s i)) (skip-spaces s (+ i 1)) i))

(defn- skip-token [s i]
  (let [c (at-byte s i)]
    (if (or (= c -1) (space? c)) i (skip-token s (+ i 1)))))

(defn- trim-right [s n] :string
  (cond
    (<= n 0) ""
    (space? (at-byte s (- n 1))) (trim-right s (- n 1))
    :else (string-substring s 0 n)))

;; Trim both ends. The oracle calls `str/trim`; POP3 status text routinely
;; arrives with a trailing CR the host's line reader did not strip.
(defn- trim [s]
  (let [i (skip-spaces s 0)]
    (trim-right (string-substring s i (string-byte-length s))
                (- (string-byte-length s) i))))

;; Digits at `i`, or -1 when there is no digit there. -1 rather than an
;; option because every caller here treats "no number" as "not a listing
;; line" and skips it, which is what the oracle's `keep` does.
(defn- read-i64 [s i acc]
  (let [c (at-byte s i)]
    (cond
      (digit? c) (read-i64 s (+ i 1) (+ (* (if (< acc 0) 0 acc) 10) (- c 48)))
      :else acc)))

(defn- parse-i64-at [s i]
  (if (digit? (at-byte s i)) (read-i64 s i -1) -1))

;; --- integers to text -----------------------------------------------------
;; `RETR <n>` puts a number on the wire and there is no integer-to-string
;; builtin, so the digits come out of a literal — the same way
;; `examples/todo-app.kotoba` reads its counter. `rem` is spelled as a
;; subtraction because `max` is in `:admitted-builtins` and still has no
;; lowering (measured 2026-08-30), and one such surprise per file is enough.
;;
;; This is the second copy of these four lines in the workspace
;; (`org-ietf-imap` has the first). They cannot be shared: the only linking
;; route is the project route, which the CLI runs on the JVM, which Q9
;; forbids — see adr-2608302100 for the measurement and the removal
;; conditions.

(defn- digit-char [n]
  (string-substring "0123456789" n (+ n 1)))

(defn- i64->string [n] :string
  (if (< n 10)
    (digit-char n)
    (string-concat (i64->string (quot n 10))
                   (digit-char (- n (* (quot n 10) 10))))))

;; --- ASCII case folding ---------------------------------------------------
;; RFC 2449 §5 makes capability names case-insensitive, and comparing them
;; literally works against one server and not the next. The oracle
;; upper-cases both sides into a map; there is no code-point-to-string
;; builtin in the guest, so this compares without constructing a string —
;; which is the same decision and one allocation fewer.

(defn- fold [c] (if (and (>= c 97) (<= c 122)) (- c 32) c))

(defn- ci-eq-from? [a ai b bi n] :bool
  (cond
    (<= n 0) true
    (not (= (fold (at-byte a ai)) (fold (at-byte b bi)))) false
    :else (ci-eq-from? a (+ ai 1) b (+ bi 1) (- n 1))))

;; Is the whitespace-delimited token starting at `ai` equal to `b`, ignoring
;; ASCII case?
(defn- ci-token=? [a ai b]
  (let [end (skip-token a ai)]
    (and (= (- end ai) (string-byte-length b))
         (ci-eq-from? a ai b 0 (string-byte-length b)))))

;; Does any whitespace-delimited token of `s` from `i` equal `b`?
(defn- ci-has-token? [s i b] :bool
  (let [start (skip-spaces s i)]
    (cond
      (= (at-byte s start) -1) false
      (ci-token=? s start b) true
      :else (ci-has-token? s (skip-token s start) b))))

;; --- status lines (RFC 1939 §3) -------------------------------------------
;; POP3 has exactly two status indicators and no numeric codes; that is the
;; whole of its error model.

(defn- ok-line? [line] (starts-with? line "+OK"))

(defn- err-line? [line] (starts-with? line "-ERR"))

(defn- status-line? [line] (or (ok-line? line) (err-line? line)))

(defn- status-text [line]
  (cond
    (ok-line? line) (trim (string-substring line 3 (string-byte-length line)))
    (err-line? line) (trim (string-substring line 4 (string-byte-length line)))
    :else ""))

(defn- terminator? [line] (string=? line "."))

;; RFC 1939 §3 byte-stuffing, undone. A client that does not remove the extra
;; dot corrupts every message containing a line starting with a period —
;; which is rarer than it sounds and therefore worse.
(defn- unstuff [line]
  (if (starts-with? line "..")
    (string-substring line 1 (string-byte-length line))
    line))

;; The `<process.clock@host>` a greeting carries when the server offers APOP
;; (RFC 1939 §7). Its presence in the greeting is APOP's ONLY advertisement —
;; there is no capability for it — so a client that discards the greeting
;; cannot know the option exists.
(defn- stamp-end [s i]
  (let [c (at-byte s i)]
    (cond (= c -1) -1
          (= c 62) i
          :else (stamp-end s (+ i 1)))))

(defn- stamp-from [s i] :string
  (let [c (at-byte s i)]
    (cond
      (= c -1) ""
      (= c 60) (let [e (stamp-end s (+ i 1))]
                 (if (< e 0) "" (string-substring s i (+ e 1))))
      :else (stamp-from s (+ i 1)))))

;; --- commands (RFC 1939 §5, RFC 2449, RFC 5034) ---------------------------

(defn- line1 [verb] (string-concat verb "\r\n"))

(defn- line2 [verb :string arg]
  (string-concat verb (string-concat " " (line1 arg))))

;; --- state constructors ---------------------------------------------------

(defn init [config]
  (document-map :phase (document-keyword :greeting)
                :user (document-string (ds config :user))
                :has-password (document-bool (db config :has-password?))
                :has-token (document-bool (db config :has-access-token?))
                :uidl-wanted (document-bool
                              (option-value-of [:option :bool]
                                               (document-bool-value (dg config :uidl?))
                                               true))
                :want (document-keyword
                       (option-value-of [:option :keyword]
                                        (document-keyword-value (dg config :want))
                                        :list))
                :number (document-i64
                         (option-value-of [:option :i64]
                                          (document-i64-value (dg config :number))
                                          0))
                :body-state (document-keyword :none)
                :body-line (document-string "")
                :has-body-line (document-bool false)
                :retrieved (document-i64 0)
                :apop-stamp (document-string "")
                :sasl (document-string "")
                :capa (document-vector)
                :messages (document-vector)
                :uids (document-vector)
                :drop-count (document-i64 0)
                :drop-size (document-i64 0)
                :acc (document-vector)
                :out (document-string "")
                :out-kind (document-keyword :none)
                :error (document-string "")))

;; `body-line?` exists because "" is a line a message can contain: RFC 2822
;; separates headers from body with an empty one. A channel that signalled
;; "no line this step" by returning "" would merge the headers into the body
;; of every message -- which the parity test caught on the first run, and
;; which no amount of staring at `body-line` alone would have shown.
(defn- clear-body [state]
  (document-assoc (document-assoc state :body-line (document-string ""))
                  :has-body-line (document-bool false)))

(defn- quiet [state]
  (clear-body
   (document-assoc (document-assoc state :out (document-string ""))
                   :out-kind (document-keyword :none))))

(defn- send-line [state next-phase line]
  (clear-body
   (document-assoc
    (document-assoc
     (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword next-phase))
     :out (document-string line))
    :out-kind (document-keyword :line))))

;; The guest names a credential it does not hold. `outgoing` stays "" — the
;; host owns the secret, the base64 (RFC 5034) and the MD5 (RFC 1939 §7).
(defn- send-credential [state next-phase kind]
  (clear-body
   (document-assoc
    (document-assoc
     (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword next-phase))
     :out (document-string ""))
    :out-kind (document-keyword kind))))

(defn- fail [state message]
  (clear-body
   (document-assoc
    (document-assoc
     (document-assoc
      (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword :failed))
      :error (document-string message))
     :out (document-string ""))
    :out-kind (document-keyword :none))))

;; --- accumulating a multi-line response -----------------------------------

(defn- reset-acc [state]
  (document-assoc state :acc (document-vector)))

(defn- push-acc [state line]
  (document-assoc state :acc
                  (document-vector-conj (dg state :acc)
                                        (document-string (unstuff line)))))

;; --- capabilities (RFC 2449) ----------------------------------------------
;; The oracle builds an upper-cased map. What the session actually decides
;; from it is which mechanism to use, so the capability lines are kept as
;; given and asked case-insensitive questions. `sasl` is the SASL line's
;; argument list, which is the one capability whose *value* matters.

(defn- capa-sasl [acc i] :string
  (cond
    (>= i (document-count acc)) ""
    (ci-token=? (at-str acc i) (skip-spaces (at-str acc i) 0) "SASL")
    (let [line (at-str acc i)]
      (string-substring line
                        (skip-spaces line (skip-token line (skip-spaces line 0)))
                        (string-byte-length line)))
    :else (capa-sasl acc (+ i 1))))

(defn- capa-has? [acc i name :string] :bool
  (cond
    (>= i (document-count acc)) false
    (ci-token=? (at-str acc i) (skip-spaces (at-str acc i) 0) name) true
    :else (capa-has? acc (+ i 1) name)))

;; --- listings (RFC 1939 §5, §7) -------------------------------------------
;; `+OK 2 320` -> count and size; `1 120` -> number and size; `1 whqtswO00`
;; -> number and UID. The oracle reaches for `re-find` three times; these are
;; the same three grammars read positionally.

(defn- stat-count [line]
  (parse-i64-at line (skip-spaces line 3)))

(defn- stat-size [line]
  (let [i (skip-spaces line 3)]
    (parse-i64-at line (skip-spaces line (skip-token line i)))))

(defn- listing-number [line]
  (parse-i64-at line (skip-spaces line 0)))

(defn- listing-second-i64 [line]
  (let [i (skip-spaces line 0)]
    (parse-i64-at line (skip-spaces line (skip-token line i)))))

(defn- listing-second-token [line]
  (let [i (skip-spaces line 0)
        s (skip-spaces line (skip-token line i))]
    (string-substring line s (skip-token line s))))

;; LIST body -> [{:number :size}]. A line without a leading number is skipped,
;; which is what the oracle's `keep` does.
(defn- scan-listing [acc i out :document] :document
  (if (>= i (document-count acc))
    out
    (let [line (at-str acc i)
          n (listing-number line)]
      (scan-listing acc (+ i 1)
                    (if (< n 0)
                      out
                      (document-vector-conj
                       out
                       (document-map :number (document-i64 n)
                                     :size (document-i64 (listing-second-i64 line))
                                     :uid (document-string ""))))))))

(defn- uidl-listing [acc i out :document] :document
  (if (>= i (document-count acc))
    out
    (let [line (at-str acc i)
          n (listing-number line)]
      (uidl-listing acc (+ i 1)
                    (if (< n 0)
                      out
                      (document-vector-conj
                       out
                       (document-map :number (document-i64 n)
                                     :uid (document-string
                                           (listing-second-token line)))))))))

;; The join RFC 1939 §7 makes necessary. LIST gives sizes against numbers
;; that do not survive the session; UIDL gives the identifier that does.
;; Neither alone is the answer a caller can cache.
(defn- uid-for [uids i number] :string
  (cond
    (>= i (document-count uids)) ""
    (= (di (at uids i) :number) number) (ds (at uids i) :uid)
    :else (uid-for uids (+ i 1) number)))

(defn- join-uids [msgs uids :document i out :document] :document
  (if (>= i (document-count msgs))
    out
    (join-uids msgs uids (+ i 1)
               (document-vector-conj
                out
                (document-assoc (at msgs i) :uid
                                (document-string
                                 (uid-for uids 0 (di (at msgs i) :number))))))))

;; --- choosing an authentication mechanism ---------------------------------
;; XOAUTH2 when a token exists, then SASL PLAIN, then APOP where the greeting
;; offered it, then USER/PASS. This is the one decision in the session that is
;; not forced by the wire: it is why a mailbox already covered by an OAuth
;; grant does not need an app password. The guest picks; the host supplies the
;; secret the pick names.

(defn- begin-auth [state]
  (let [sasl (ds state :sasl)
        pass (db state :has-password)
        token (db state :has-token)]
    (cond
      (and token (ci-has-token? sasl 0 "XOAUTH2"))
      (send-line state :auth-xoauth2-init (line2 "AUTH" "XOAUTH2"))

      (and pass (ci-has-token? sasl 0 "PLAIN"))
      (send-line state :auth-plain-init (line2 "AUTH" "PLAIN"))

      ;; APOP digests MD5 over the greeting stamp and the secret. Choosing it
      ;; is the decision; `apop-timestamp` tells the host what to digest.
      (and pass (not (string=? (ds state :apop-stamp) "")))
      (send-credential state :apop :credential/apop)

      pass (send-line state :user (line2 "USER" (ds state :user)))

      :else (fail state "POP3 の認証情報がありません。"))))

;; --- after authentication -------------------------------------------------

(defn- begin-stat [state]
  (send-line state :stat (line1 "STAT")))

(defn- begin-list [state]
  (send-line (reset-acc state) :list (line1 "LIST")))

;; RETR is the other half of what a POP3 client is for. The number goes on
;; the wire; the message that comes back does not come in here.
(defn- begin-retr [state]
  (send-line state :retr
             (line2 "RETR" (i64->string (di state :number)))))

(defn- begin-work [state]
  (if (= (dk state :want) :retrieve)
    (begin-retr state)
    (begin-list state)))

(defn- after-list [state]
  (let [msgs (scan-listing (dg state :acc) 0 (document-vector))
        kept (document-assoc state :messages msgs)]
    (if (db state :uidl-wanted)
      (send-line (reset-acc kept) :uidl (line1 "UIDL"))
      (send-line kept :quit (line1 "QUIT")))))

(defn- after-uidl [state]
  (let [uids (uidl-listing (dg state :acc) 0 (document-vector))]
    (send-line
     (document-assoc (document-assoc state :uids uids)
                     :messages (join-uids (dg state :messages) uids 0 (document-vector)))
     :quit (line1 "QUIT"))))

;; --- transitions ----------------------------------------------------------

(defn- on-status [state line] :document
  (let [p (dk state :phase)
        ok (ok-line? line)
        text (status-text line)]
    (cond
      (= p :greeting)
      (if ok
        (send-line (document-assoc state :apop-stamp
                                   (document-string (stamp-from line 0)))
                   :capa (line1 "CAPA"))
        (fail state "POP3 greeting was not +OK"))

      ;; RFC 2449: a server without CAPA answers -ERR, and that is not a
      ;; failure worth unwinding a session over.
      (= p :capa)
      (if ok (quiet (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword :capa-body)))
          (begin-auth state))

      ;; RFC 5034: the success line and the continuation both begin with
      ;; `+` — `+OK` against a bare `+ ` — so success is tested first.
      ;; Testing the `+` prefix first sends the credentials a second time,
      ;; in answer to the server saying they were accepted, and then blocks
      ;; on a reply that is never coming.
      (= p :auth-plain-init)
      (cond
        ok (begin-stat state)
        (err-line? line)
        (fail state (string-concat "POP3 AUTH PLAIN failed: " text))
        (starts-with? line "+")
        (send-credential state :auth-plain-payload :credential/sasl-plain)
        :else (quiet state))

      (= p :auth-xoauth2-init)
      (cond
        ok (begin-stat state)
        (err-line? line)
        (fail state (string-concat "POP3 AUTH XOAUTH2 failed: " text))
        (starts-with? line "+")
        (send-credential state :auth-xoauth2-payload :credential/sasl-xoauth2)
        :else (quiet state))

      (= p :auth-plain-payload)
      (cond
        ok (begin-stat state)
        (err-line? line) (fail state (string-concat "POP3 AUTH PLAIN failed: " text))
        :else (quiet state))

      (= p :auth-xoauth2-payload)
      (cond
        ok (begin-stat state)
        (err-line? line) (fail state (string-concat "POP3 AUTH XOAUTH2 failed: " text))
        :else (quiet state))

      (= p :apop)
      (if ok (begin-stat state)
          (fail state (string-concat "POP3 APOP failed: " text)))

      (= p :user)
      (if ok (send-credential state :pass :credential/pass)
          (fail state (string-concat "POP3 USER failed: " text)))

      (= p :pass)
      (if ok (begin-stat state)
          (fail state (string-concat "POP3 PASS failed: " text)))

      (= p :stat)
      (if ok
        (begin-work
         (document-assoc
          (document-assoc state :drop-count (document-i64 (let [v (stat-count line)] (if (< v 0) 0 v))))
          :drop-size (document-i64 (let [v (stat-size line)] (if (< v 0) 0 v)))))
        (fail state (string-concat "POP3 STAT failed: " text)))

      (= p :list)
      (if ok (quiet (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword :list-body)))
          (fail state (string-concat "POP3 LIST failed: " text)))

      ;; RFC 1939 §3 sends the body only on success, so reading for a
      ;; terminator after -ERR waits for a line that is never coming.
      (= p :retr)
      (if ok
        (quiet (document-assoc
                (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword :retr-body))
                :body-state (document-keyword :open)))
        (fail state (string-concat "POP3 RETR failed: " text)))

      ;; UIDL is optional (RFC 1939 §7). Its absence leaves the caller with
      ;; numbers only, which is an absent :uid rather than a failed listing.
      (= p :uidl)
      (if ok (quiet (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword :uidl-body)))
          (send-line state :quit (line1 "QUIT")))

      ;; QUIT moves the server into UPDATE state and releases the maildrop
      ;; lock. The oracle swallows a failed QUIT because the transport closes
      ;; either way; so does this.
      (= p :quit) (quiet (document-assoc state :phase (document-keyword :done)))

      :else (quiet state))))

(defn- on-body [state line :string]
  (let [p (dk state :phase)]
    (cond
      ;; The message is handed over one unstuffed line at a time and kept
      ;; nowhere. Only the count survives in the guest.
      (and (= p :retr-body) (not (terminator? line)))
      (document-assoc
       (document-assoc
        (document-assoc
         (document-assoc (quiet state) :body-line (document-string (unstuff line)))
         :has-body-line (document-bool true))
        :retrieved (document-i64 (+ (di state :retrieved) 1)))
       :body-state (document-keyword :open))

      (= p :retr-body)
      (send-line (document-assoc state :body-state (document-keyword :closed))
                 :quit (line1 "QUIT"))

      (not (terminator? line)) (quiet (push-acc state line))

      (= p :capa-body)
      (begin-auth
       (document-assoc
        (document-assoc state :capa (dg state :acc))
        :sasl (document-string (capa-sasl (dg state :acc) 0))))

      (= p :list-body) (after-list state)
      (= p :uidl-body) (after-uidl state)
      :else (quiet state))))

(defn- body-phase? [p :keyword]
  (or (= p :capa-body)
      (or (= p :list-body) (or (= p :uidl-body) (= p :retr-body)))))

;; RFC 5034's AUTH exchange is not made of status lines: the server's
;; request for the initial response is a bare `+`. Applying the status-line
;; grammar to it reports a protocol violation on a conforming server.
(defn- auth-phase? [p :keyword]
  (or (or (= p :auth-plain-init) (= p :auth-xoauth2-init))
      (or (= p :auth-plain-payload) (= p :auth-xoauth2-payload))))

(defn step [state line :string] :document
  (let [p (dk state :phase)]
    (cond
      (= p :done) (quiet state)
      (= p :failed) (quiet state)
      (body-phase? p) (on-body state line)
      (auth-phase? p) (on-status state line)
      (not (status-line? line)) (fail state "unparseable POP3 status line")
      :else (on-status state line))))

;; EOF mid-response. The oracle throws "POP3 connection closed mid-response";
;; a closed socket is a host fact, so the host reports it and the guest owns
;; what it means for the session.
(defn closed [state]
  (let [p (dk state :phase)]
    (cond
      (= p :done) (quiet state)
      (= p :failed) (quiet state)
      :else (fail state "POP3 connection closed mid-response"))))

;; --- projections ----------------------------------------------------------

(defn phase [state] (dk state :phase))

(defn outgoing [state] (ds state :out))

(defn outgoing-kind [state] (dk state :out-kind))

(defn error-text [state] (ds state :error))

;; :none -> :open -> :closed. The host opens a buffer on :open, appends every
;; non-empty `body-line`, and seals it on :closed. Nothing here holds the
;; message.
(defn body-state [state] (dk state :body-state))

;; Did this step produce a body line? Ask before reading `body-line`: ""
;; is a line a message can contain (RFC 2822's header/body separator), so
;; the emptiness of the string cannot carry "there was none".
(defn body-line? [state] (db state :has-body-line))

;; The unstuffed line the host should append. Valid only for the step that
;; produced it, and only when `body-line?`.
(defn body-line [state] (ds state :body-line))

;; How many body lines went past. The caller asked for a message and is owed
;; an answer about it; that answer costs one i64, not the message.
(defn retrieved-bytes [state] (di state :retrieved))

(defn apop-timestamp [state] (ds state :apop-stamp))

(defn drop-count [state] (di state :drop-count))

(defn drop-size [state] (di state :drop-size))

(defn message-count [state]
  (document-count (dg state :messages)))

(defn message-number-at [state i]
  (di (at (dg state :messages) i) :number))

(defn message-size-at [state i]
  (di (at (dg state :messages) i) :size))

;; "" where the oracle answers nil: the guest has no nil, and a server
;; without UIDL is the ordinary case, not an error.
(defn message-uid-at [state i]
  (ds (at (dg state :messages) i) :uid))

(defn supports? [state name :string]
  (capa-has? (dg state :capa) 0 name))

(defn sasl-plain? [state]
  (ci-has-token? (ds state :sasl) 0 "PLAIN"))

(defn sasl-xoauth2? [state]
  (ci-has-token? (ds state :sasl) 0 "XOAUTH2"))
